## Introducing This Project

I deployed an insecure web app (OWASP Juice Shop) with CloudFormation and attacked it to see how GuardDuty detects real attack techniques. I bypassed the login with SQL injection, used command injection to expose the EC2 instance's credentials, then used those credentials in CloudShell to steal a secret file from an S3 bucket. GuardDuty reported the credential exfiltration within 10 minutes, and I investigated the finding to trace what was accessed and from where.

### Tools and Concepts

GuardDuty, CloudFormation, EC2, S3, CloudShell, SQL injection, command injection, input sanitization, credential exfiltration, and GuardDuty Malware Protection.

### Project Reflection

The most challenging part was getting access to the victim's AWS environment using a profile built from the exposed credentials. It was most rewarding to retrieve the secret file from the victim's S3 bucket and then see the same activity show up as a GuardDuty finding, UnauthorizedAccess:IAMUser/InstanceCredentialExfiltration.InsideAWS, which showed the S3 GetObject call and the attacker's IP address and location. As an extension, I enabled Malware Protection and uploaded an EICAR test file, which instantly triggered an Object:S3/MaliciousFile finding.

